```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
```
prints 3 3 3, want 0 1 2

response:
`var` is function-scoped, so all three callbacks share one `i`, which is 3 by the time they run. `let` creates a fresh binding for each iteration:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
```

prompt:
```js
async function saveAll(items) {
    items.forEach(async (item) => {
        await save(item);
    });
    console.log("done");
}
```
"done" prints before anything is saved

response:
`forEach` ignores the promises returned by its async callback, so it finishes immediately and the function moves on. Use a loop that awaits each save in order:

```js
async function saveAll(items) {
    for (const item of items) {
        await save(item);
    }
    console.log("done");
}
```

If the saves are independent and can run together, use `await Promise.all(items.map(save))` instead.

